  HUMAN FINDINGS GATE
============================================================

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  All three hypotheses about BLIS's Always-Disaggregate (AD) vs Always-Local (AL) inference strategies were confirmed across 54 simulation runs (9 arrival rates × 3 seeds × 2 modes). AD consistently lowers mean inter-token latency (ITL) at the cost of higher time-to-first-token (TTFT), and this tradeoff improves at higher request rates.

  * h-main CONFIRMED: AD produces lower ITL than AL at all 27 rate-seed combinations. The ITL gap grows from +0.058ms at 5 req/s to +1.687ms at 150 req/s. AD's TTFT penalty over AL is confirmed at all combinations, ranging from +9.61ms (rate=5) to +4.43ms (rate=120).
  * h-dose-response CONFIRMED: Mean ITL increases monotonically with arrival rate for both AL and AD across all seeds. Growth decelerates above 60 req/s (concave shape), which the designer correctly predicted as a transient artifact of the 300-request finite horizon preventing steady-state at high rates.
  * h-tradeoff CONFIRMED: AD's TTFT cost stays well within the 15ms budget at every rate-seed pair (max observed: ~10ms at rate=5). Unexpected but explainable finding: the tradeoff gets better at higher rates — ITL savings grow while TTFT cost shrinks, because AL's TTFT inflates under decode-pool congestion while AD's TTFT is bounded by the lightly-loaded prefill pipeline.
  * All 54 runs completed 300/300 requests with zero drops. No discrepancies from predictions. The experiment is internally valid and results are robust across seeds.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

