────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Iteration 3 tests whether the paper's closed-form ITL formula (ITL = α/(1−ρ)) quantitatively predicts BLIS simulator output across arrival rates 5–150 req/s, or whether the simulator's finite-horizon (300 requests) transient behavior produces a fundamentally different (approximately linear) ITL-vs-rate curve. This escalates from iter-2's confirmed directional validation to precise quantitative formula fitting.

  * 54 total runs (9 arrival rates × 2 PD modes AL/AD × 3 seeds) using fixed parameters (512 prompt tokens, 128 output tokens, 4 instances, 300 requests). Each run takes <3s wall time, so total experiment completes in ~3 minutes.
  * h-main predicts the paper's 1/(1−ρ) formula will fail quantitatively (R² < 0.95) across the full rate range because the data is approximately linear (transient regime), while a simple linear model will achieve R² > 0.95. Also predicts the fitted per-request work parameter w_AL > w_AD, consistent with the paper's claim that aggregated-local processing adds more per-request overhead than disaggregated decode-only.
  * h-dose-response predicts the formula's overprediction percentage (calibrated at low rates) is monotone increasing with arrival rate — under 1% at rate=20, exceeding 30% (AD) and 50% (AL) at rate=150 — confirming the transient-regime mechanism rather than random noise drives divergence.
  * h-control-negative predicts the formula IS accurate (MAPE < 1%) at low rates (5, 10, 20 req/s) where 300/rate gives enough simulation time (15–60s) for steady-state convergence, validating the formula's structure in its applicable regime.
  * Key diagnostics are well-specified: if the formula unexpectedly fits well (R² ≥ 0.95), check seed variance masking shape mismatch; if residuals are non-monotone, check for KV cache saturation or Poisson variance effects; if low-rate MAPE > 1%, check calibration point separation and cold-start bias.
  * Design is sound and efficient — all three arms share the same 54 runs with different analysis passes. Parameters are carefully audited (KV blocks, timeout, scheduler all at safe defaults). The experiment cleanly isolates whether the formula's steady-state assumption is the specific mechanism causing quantitative mismatch.

