────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  We are testing whether the paper's ITL formula (1/(1-ρ) form) breaks down at high request rates because of a fundamental structural mismatch with BLIS's latency model (nonlinear batch-size terms in StepTime), rather than simply because of finite-horizon transient effects (ramp-up/drain phases). This is iteration 4, deepening the causal diagnosis from earlier directional findings by analyzing per-request ITL quartiles across 9 rates × 2 PD modes × 3 seeds (54 runs).

  * h-main: Restricting to Q2-Q3 requests (middle 50% by arrival order) removes transient ramp-up/drain phases. Prediction: this WORSENS the formula's R² fit (R²_full − R²_Q2Q3 ≥ 0.02 in at least one mode), proving the mismatch is structural — the steady-state data is MORE concave than the convex 1/(1-ρ) form allows. If R² instead improves, the breakdown is transient-driven and the formula's structural form is fine.
  * h-control-negative: At low rates (≤10 req/s), Q2-Q3 mean ITL should differ from full-run mean by <0.2%, confirming transients are negligible when simulation duration >> service time. This bounds the regime where the formula's steady-state assumption holds.
  * h-dose-response: ITL intra-run spread (max minus min quartile mean, normalized) should follow an inverted-U across rates 5–150, peaking at 80–120 req/s. Probed values support this (0.1% at rate=5, 12.1% at rate=100, 9.2% at rate=150). If monotone increasing, the drain-phase ITL suppression at very high rates is weaker than expected.
