============================================================
  HUMAN FINDINGS GATE
============================================================

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  All three arms confirmed: the 1/(1-ρ) queueing formula's poor fit to BLIS ITL data is structural, not caused by simulation transients. Removing ramp-up/drain transients (using only Q2-Q3 quartile data) actually worsened the formula fit (R² dropped by 0.044 for AL mode and 0.023 for AD mode), because the simulator's quasi-steady-state ITL grows sub-linearly (concave) with load while the formula is convex. A simple linear fit consistently outperforms the queueing formula (R²=0.976–0.985 vs 0.919–0.947).

  * Main hypothesis CONFIRMED: R² difference between full-run and Q2-Q3 fits exceeded the 0.02 threshold in both modes (AL: 0.0438, AD: 0.0231). The formula fits worse on transient-free data, proving the mismatch is structural — BLIS's batch-size-dependent terms (sumCtx in tDcKv, MoE nEff scaling) create concave ITL growth that the convex 1/(1-ρ) formula cannot capture.
  * Negative control CONFIRMED: At low rates (≤10 req/s), Q2-Q3 vs full-run ITL differences were negligible (<0.1%) and quartile means were nearly identical (e.g., AL rate=5: all quartiles within 0.006ms), validating that transients are irrelevant at low load and the steady-state assumption holds there.
  * Dose-response CONFIRMED: ITL intra-run spread follows an inverted-U across rates 5–150 req/s, peaking at rate=80 for AL (12.15%) and rate=100 for AD (10.47%) — both within the predicted 80–120 range. The peak location difference is explained by AD's dedicated decode instances lowering per-instance arrival rate.
  * Practical implication: The linear model (R²≈0.98) is a better approximation of BLIS ITL behavior than the queueing-theoretic 1/(1-ρ) formula (R²≈0.93). Any analytical model for BLIS must account for batch-size-dependent service time scaling to be accurate at moderate-to-high loads.

